# EXAMPLE authorized_keys for the relay sshd — DO NOT USE AS-IS.
#
# The relay container reads /home/tunnel/.ssh/authorized_keys (see sshd_config)
# and start.sh writes this file from TUNNEL_AUTHORIZED_KEYS_B64 (preferred) or
# TUNNEL_AUTHORIZED_KEYS. Replace each placeholder public key below with the
# real public half of the corresponding private key, one line per key, then put
# the whole block into TUNNEL_AUTHORIZED_KEYS on Railway and redeploy.
#
# The relay's sshd listens on 127.0.0.1:22 only (loopback), password auth is
# disabled, the only user is "tunnel", and there is no shell or PTY for these
# keys. Each key is locked down to exactly one thing on port 2222.

# 1. QNX-to-relay tunnel key (private half lives on the QNX Pi, in
#    /data/home/root/.ssh/qnx_tunnel_key after first boot). permitlisten lets
#    the Pi open the loopback reverse listener on 127.0.0.1:2222 and nothing
#    else.
restrict,port-forwarding,permitlisten="127.0.0.1:2222" ssh-ed25519 AAAA_REPLACE_WITH_QNX_TUNNEL_PUBLIC_KEY qnxpi-tunnel

# 2. Laptop-to-relay access key (private half lives on your laptop).
#    permitopen lets the laptop connect to the Pi's 127.0.0.1:2222 listener
#    and nothing else. This key is only for reaching the relay; it is NOT
#    authorized to log in to the QNX Pi itself.
restrict,port-forwarding,permitopen="127.0.0.1:2222" ssh-ed25519 AAAA_REPLACE_WITH_LAPTOP_RELAY_PUBLIC_KEY laptop-relay@your-machine
